Article 5
Every Woman has the right to:
a. obtain protection from discriminatory actions;
b. obtain protection from economic exploitation and sexual exploitation;
c. obtain protection from physical violence, psychological violence, sexual violence, and neglect;
d. obtain decent work in accordance with her capabilities;
e. obtain work leave while receiving wages/salary in accordance with statutory regulations;
f. obtain education and teaching in all types, levels and pathways of education;
g. receive special protection in the implementation of work or profession against matters that may threaten safety and/or health related to Women's reproductive function;
h. have the right to choose, be chosen, be appointed in work, positions, and professions in accordance with requirements and statutory regulations; and
i. ownership and management of joint assets in marriage in accordance with the provisions of Statutory Regulations.

Article 3
The Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children aims to:
a. serve as a guideline for the Regency Government in planning, policies and strategies for the Protection of Women and Children;
b. protect Women and Children from actions, decisions and policies that violate the rights of Women and Children;
c. prevent violations of the Rights of Women and Children;
d. increase empowerment of Women and Children;
e. enhance the role and value of local wisdom in the Protection of Women and Children; and
f. enhance the role of Regional Apparatus and non-Government entities in the Regency in efforts to Protect Women and Children.

Article 2
(1) The implementation of Protection of Women is based on the principles of:
a. the best interests of Women;
b. gender justice and gender equality;
c. local wisdom; and
d. legal certainty.
(2) The implementation of Protection of Children is based on the principles of:
a. non-discrimination;
b. the best interests of the Child;
c. the right to life, survival, and development;
d. respect for the views of the Child; and
e. legal certainty.

Article 4
The scope of regulation for the Protection of Women and Children includes:
a. prevention;
b. implementation of Protection of the Rights of Women and Children;
c. handling;
d. empowerment; and
e. guidance and supervision.

Article 11
The responsibility of the family in efforts to protect women includes:
a. protecting women from disputes and violence in the household that degrade the dignity of women;
b. providing equal opportunities to women to develop themselves in the fields of:
1. education;
2. social affairs;
3. economics; and
4. politics and culture.
c. preventing violations of women's rights in the household.

Article 7
(1) The Regency Government in efforts to protect Women is obligated and responsible to:
a. guarantee Women's Rights without discrimination in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations;
b. establish and implement policy programs and activities for the protection of Women;
c. cooperate with the community and/or Community Organizations in the implementation of Women's Protection in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations;
d. conduct rehabilitation and empowerment of Women who are victims of acts of violence;
e. provide special protection to Women victims of acts of violence and human trafficking; and
f. take preventive action in order to prevent violations of Women's rights.
(2) The responsibility of the Regency Government in efforts to protect Women's Rights as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented by DPPKBP-PPPA.

Article 15
(1) Efforts made by the family in order to prevent violations of Women's rights include:
a. protection of women from various violations of rights in the household; and
b. encouraging the formation of harmonious, educative and democratic families;
(2) Efforts made by parents in order to prevent violations of Children's rights include:
a. providing good examples to children in accordance with religious teachings and customs;
b. supervision of children in their activities inside - 17 - or outside the home;
c. providing opportunities for children to dialogue and express opinions democratically; and
d. protection of children from various acts of violation of Children's rights in the household.

Article 13
(1) Efforts undertaken by the Regency Government in order to prevent violations against the rights of Women include:
a. conducting socialization regarding the prevention of violence against Women by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. providing guidelines on handling acts of violence against Women;
c. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration;
d. developing an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against Women;
e. developing family empowerment programs;
f. strengthening the function of community organizations working in the field of Women's empowerment; and
g. providing public facilities that are comfortable and safe for Women.
(2) Efforts undertaken by the Regency Government in order to prevent violations of the rights of Children include:
a. preventing acts of violence against Children by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. developing child-friendly schools;
c. encouraging increased participation of children through the establishment of Children's Forums;
d. providing guidelines on handling acts of violence against Children;
e. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration services for child victims of violence;
f. developing an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against Children;
g. strengthening the function of community organizations working in the field of Child protection; and
h. encouraging the establishment of Child-Friendly Regencies.

Article 12
(1) Parents, in efforts to protect Children, are responsible for:
a. caring for, maintaining, educating, and protecting Children;
b. developing Children in accordance with their abilities, talents, and interests;
c. providing formal and non-formal education;
d. guiding Children to behave in accordance with religion, ethics and morals as well as customs; and
e. preventing the occurrence of marriage at a child's age.
(2) In the event that parents do not exist, or their whereabouts are unknown, or due to a certain reason, cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) may be transferred to the family.

Article 48
(1) Every Woman and Child victim of violence is provided spiritual guidance aimed at psychological recovery.
(2) Spiritual guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented based on the principles of:
a. not imposing any religion or belief; and
b. leaving the final decision in the hands of the victim (self determination).
(3) Spiritual guidance as referred to in paragraph (2) is conducted by spiritual guidance officers.
(4) Spiritual guidance officers as referred to in paragraph (3) consist of:
a. consultants of the Marriage Advisory, Development and Preservation Agency (BP4);
b. Officers of the Office of Religious Affairs (KUA);
c. Ustad or Ulama;
d. Pastor;
e. Reverend; and
f. other religious leaders who have gender sensitivity or gender awareness.

Article 6
(1) Every Child has the right to:
a. every child has the right to protection by parents, family, community, and the state;
b. the rights of the child are human rights and for their interests the rights of the child are recognized and protected by law even since in the womb;
c. every child since in the womb, has the right to live, survive, and improve their standard of living;
d. every child since birth, has the right to a name and citizenship status;
e. every child with physical and/or mental disabilities has the right to obtain care, education, training, and special assistance at state expense, to ensure their life in accordance with human dignity, increase self-confidence, and the ability to participate in community, national, and state life;
f. every child has the right to worship according to their religion, think, express themselves in accordance with their level of intellectuality and age under the guidance of parents and/or guardians;
g. every child has the right to know who their parents are, to be raised, and cared for by their own parents;
h. in the event that the child's parents are unable to raise and care for their child properly, then the child may be cared for or adopted as a child by another person in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
i. every child has the right to be raised, maintained, cared for, educated, directed, and guided in their life by parents or guardians until adulthood in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
j. every child has the right to obtain adoptive parents or a guardian based on a court decision if both parents have died or due to a valid reason cannot carry out their obligations as parents;
k. adoptive parents or guardians as referred to in letter j must carry out the obligations as actual parents;
l. every child has the right to obtain legal protection from all forms of physical or mental violence, neglect, maltreatment, and sexual abuse while in the care of parents or guardians, or other parties responsible for the care of the child;
m. in the event that parents, guardians, or child caregivers commit any form of physical or mental abuse, neglect, maltreatment, and sexual abuse including rape, and/or murder of a child who should be protected, then aggravated punishment must be imposed;
n. every child has the right not to be separated from their parents against the child's own will, unless there are valid legal reasons and rules showing that the separation is in the best interests of the child;
o. in the circumstances as referred to in letter n, the child's right to continue to meet directly and maintain personal contact regularly with their parents remains guaranteed by law;
p. every child has the right to obtain education and teaching in the context of personal development in accordance with their interests, talents, and level of intelligence;
q. every child has the right to seek, receive, and provide information in accordance with their level of intellectuality and age for their self-development as long as it is in accordance with moral values and propriety;
r. every child has the right to rest, associate with peers, play, recreate, and create in accordance with their interests, talents, and level of intelligence for their self-development;
s. every child has the right to obtain health services and social security appropriately, in accordance with their physical and spiritual mental needs;
t. every child has the right not to be involved in war events, armed conflicts, social unrest, and other events containing elements of violence;
u. every child has the right to obtain protection from economic exploitation activities and any work that endangers them, so as to disrupt their education, physical health, morals, social life, and spiritual mental well-being;
v. every child has the right to obtain protection from exploitation activities and sexual abuse, kidnapping, child trafficking, as well as from various forms of narcotics, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
w. every child has the right not to be subjected to persecution, torture, or imposition of inhuman punishment;
x. every child has the right not to be deprived of freedom unlawfully;
y. arrest, detention, or imprisonment of a child may only be carried out in accordance with applicable law and can only be implemented as a last resort;
z. every child who is deprived of freedom has the right to be treated humanely and with attention to the needs of personal development according to their age and must be separated from adults, except in their best interests;
aa. every child who is deprived of freedom has the right to obtain legal assistance or other assistance effectively at every stage of applicable legal remedies;
bb. every child who is deprived of freedom has the right to defend themselves and obtain justice before an objective and impartial Juvenile Court in a closed hearing to the public.
(2) Every child who becomes a victim or perpetrator of sexual violence or who is in conflict with the law has the right to confidentiality.
(3) Every Child with Disabilities in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in paragraph (1) is also entitled to obtain:
a. special needs education/special education;
b. rehabilitation;
c. social assistance; and
d. maintenance of social welfare standards.
(4) Every Child who has excellence has the right to receive special education.

REGARDING
PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN
BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD
THE REGENT OF OGAN KOMERING ULU SELATAN,

Considering : a. that the government has the obligation to provide protection, advancement, enforcement and fulfillment of Human Rights of every citizen including the Rights of Women and Children;
b. that Women and Children are vulnerable groups who tend to experience violence and discrimination and therefore need to receive optimal protection;
c. that the fulfillment of constitutional rights of Women and Children as well as the improvement of the quality of life of Women and Children, is one of the mandatory affairs which is the duty, authority and responsibility of the Regency Government;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children;

In View of : 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 39 of 1999 on Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
3. Law Number 23 of 2002 on Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235), as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 17 of 2016 on the Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 on the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 on Child Protection into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 237, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5946);
4. Law Number 37 of 2003 on the Establishment of East Ogan Komering Ulu Regency, South Ogan Komering Ulu Regency and Ogan Ilir Regency in South Sumatra Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 152, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4347);
5. Law Number 23 of 2004 on the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
6. Law Number 21 of 2007 on the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
7. Law Number 23 of 2014 on Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as has been amended several times, most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 on the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 on Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
8. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 on the Implementation and Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
9. Government Regulation Number 59 of 2019 on the Implementation of Coordination of Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2019 Number 160, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6382);
10. Regulation of the Minister of Women's Empowerment Number 1 of 2010 on Minimum Service Standards in the Field of Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 483);
11. Regulation of the State Minister of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 15 of 2010 on General Guidelines for Handling Children in Conflict with the Law (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 483);
12. Regulation of the State Minister of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 on Guidelines for the Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 903);

With Mutual Agreement

Article 41
(1) The implementation of handling of Women and Children victims of violence as referred to in Article 40 is carried out through integrated services.
(2) Integrated services for Women and Children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) use the principles of:
a. gender responsive;
b. non-discrimination;
c. equal and respectful relationships;
d. maintaining confidentiality;
e. providing a sense of security and comfort;
f. respecting individual differences;
g. non-judgmental;
h. respecting the choices and decisions of victims;
i. sensitive to the background and condition of victims and the use of appropriate language that is understood by victims;
j. fast and simple;
k. empathy;
l. fulfillment of the rights of Women and Children; and
m. free of charge.

Article 35
(1) Every person is prohibited from employing and involving Children in the worst forms of work.
(2) The worst forms of work as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. all work in the form of slavery or similar practices;
b. all work that exploits, provides, or offers Children for: 1. prostitution;
2. production of pornography;
3. pornographic performances;
4. gambling;
5. production and trade of alcoholic beverages; 6. narcotics;
7. psychotropic substances; and
8. other addictive substances; and/or
c. all work that endangers the health, safety, or morals of Children.

Article 5
Every Woman has the right to:
a. obtain protection from discriminatory actions;
b. obtain protection from economic exploitation and sexual exploitation;
c. obtain protection from physical violence, psychological violence, sexual violence, and neglect;
d. obtain decent work in accordance with her capabilities;
